course: dictionary
lesson: 12862
title: Enjoin: a formal order or a court's ban
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Enjoin is an old and formal word. It means to order someone to do something, or to strongly urge them to do it. «The teacher enjoined the class to stay quiet during the test.» The teacher gave a firm order, not a polite request.
explain: You will often meet enjoin in church writing, formal rules and law. «The club's rules enjoin every member to pay on time.» The rules are not asking nicely. They are telling members what they must do.
explain: In law, enjoin has a special meaning, especially in the USA. A court can enjoin a person or company from doing something, which means the court officially forbids it. «The judge enjoined the shop from selling the game until the case was over.» Clues like judge, court and case show you it is a legal order.
explain: Look for the pattern. You can enjoin someone to do something, or enjoin someone from doing something. If you can swap in the word order and the sentence still makes sense, you are probably right. If you are not sure, check a dictionary.
fact: Enjoin means to order someone to do something, or to strongly urge them to do it.
fact: In law, to enjoin someone from doing something means a court forbids it with an official order, called an injunction.
fact: Enjoin is a formal word, so you will meet it more in rules, religious writing and the news than in everyday talk.
fact: Its forms are enjoin, enjoins, enjoined and enjoining. For example, «The sign enjoined visitors to keep off the grass.»
fact: Two common patterns are enjoin someone to do something and enjoin someone from doing something.
exercise: «The sign enjoined visitors to keep off the grass.» What does enjoined mean here? | *Ordered or strongly urged | Forgot about | Carried away | Invited to a party
exercise: «The court enjoined the company from selling the game.» What does this mean? | The company was cheered by the crowd | The company must sell it quickly | *The court officially forbade the company from selling it | The company was given a prize
exercise: Which word fits the gap? «The coach ___ the team to stay calm.» | annoyed | enjoyed | joined | *enjoined
test: «The rules enjoin every member to pay on time.» Enjoin here means: | Count up carefully | *Order or strongly urge | Make a joke about | Forget about
test: The judge enjoined Ben from entering the school grounds. What did the judge do? | Asked Ben to tidy the classroom | Gave Ben a medal for being good | *Officially forbade Ben from going in | Made Ben the new head teacher
exam: Which sentence uses enjoin correctly, meaning a formal order? | *The manager enjoined the staff to lock the doors at night | I enjoin my lunch every single day | The bird enjoined a song in the tree | The river enjoined the hills
